\lispPackageChapter{sigma/os}

The \texttt{sigma/os} package provides a few helpers for talking to the
operating system: reading files and running short scripts in other languages.
The script runners use \textsc{Sbcl}'s \texttt{sb-ext:run-program} and are
convenience wrappers, not full foreign interfaces.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{perl}

The \texttt{perl} function runs a short Perl snippet by invoking the interpreter
at \texttt{*perl-path*} with \texttt{-e}.  The arguments are concatenated into a
single string of source before they are passed to Perl.  Standard output is
inherited from Lisp.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(perl \&rest \lispVar{code})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(perl "print 2 + 2, qq(\\n);")
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{python}

The \texttt{python} function runs a short Python snippet by invoking the
interpreter at \texttt{*python-path*} with \texttt{-c}.  The arguments are
concatenated into a single string of source before they are passed to Python.
Standard output is inherited from Lisp.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(python \&rest \lispVar{code})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(python "print(2 + 2)")
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{read-file}

The \texttt{read-file} function reads the entire contents of a file and returns
them as a single string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(read-file \lispVar{filename})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{filename}] A pathname designator for an existing file.
\end{description}

\lispReturns

A string containing the full contents of the file.

\lispFunctionSubsection{read-lines}

The \texttt{read-lines} function reads the entire contents of a file and returns
a list of its lines, without the terminating newlines.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(read-lines \lispVar{filename})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{filename}] A pathname designator for an existing file.
\end{description}

\lispReturns

A list of strings, one per line.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ruby}

The \texttt{ruby} function runs a short Ruby snippet by invoking the interpreter
at \texttt{*ruby-path*} with \texttt{-e}.  The arguments are concatenated into a
single string of source before they are passed to Ruby.  Standard output is
inherited from Lisp.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(ruby \&rest \lispVar{code})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ruby "puts 2 + 2")
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Parameters}

\lispParameterSubsection{*perl-path*}

The \texttt{*perl-path*} parameter holds the pathname of the Perl interpreter
used by \texttt{perl}.  The default is \texttt{/usr/bin/perl}.

\lispParameterSubsection{*python-path*}

The \texttt{*python-path*} parameter holds the pathname of the Python
interpreter used by \texttt{python}.  The default is \texttt{/usr/bin/python}.

\lispParameterSubsection{*ruby-path*}

The \texttt{*ruby-path*} parameter holds the pathname of the Ruby interpreter
used by \texttt{ruby}.  The default is \texttt{/usr/bin/ruby}.
